From 6f479bb4cffb05143d69bb261eb8c9889d32eb59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 17:15:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Seven skilling outfits you can actually find

All 47 skilling outfit pieces were imported, six outfits had their experience bonus wired in
outfit_xp.rs2, and NOT ONE PIECE COULD BE OBTAINED BY ANY MEANS. The sweep shipped in the last
commit is what found it. This gives seven of the eleven a source.

THEY ARE NOT OSRS'S SOURCES AND CANNOT BE. OSRS earns these in Motherlode Mine, the Fishing
Trawler, Temple Trekking, Wintertodt, Guardians of the Rift, the Giants' Foundry and Mahogany
Homes. None of those exists here and most is a whole minigame. So a piece is found while TRAINING
THE SKILL ITSELF, which is the nearest honest thing and needs no new content - Corey's choice of
the three offered.

THE ROLL RIDES ON THE EXPERIENCE PROC, which is the part worth keeping. Every repeatable action in
these skills already routes its experience through ~mining_xp, ~fishing_xp and the rest so the
outfit's own bonus can be applied - so that proc already sees every action, with the amount it was
worth. One line in each is the whole hook, and every rock, fish, log, fire, rune, bar and piece of
furniture now rolls without anything else being touched.

The chance is proportional to the experience - random(^outfit_roll_xp) < $xp - so a magic log rolls
twenty times as often as a normal one because it is worth twenty times as much. A flat chance per
action would have paid best for the fastest, worst action. 250,000 is one piece per 250,000
experience, about a million for a set, and it is the only number in the mechanism.

CONSTRUCTION HAD NO SUCH PROC, which is why the carpenter's outfit had nothing to hang on -
outfit_xp.rs2 said in its own header that the outfit was left out because "Construction and Hunter
do not exist in this build". Construction shipped three weeks ago. That is the second stale comment
in a week to be hiding real content, after the Garden Centre's note about the flowers. There is a
~construction_xp now and genfurn.py writes the call, so a regenerate keeps it.

AND TWENTY SKILLING AWARDS WENT ROUND THE FUNNEL ENTIRELY - the jungle tree, the Kharidian cactus,
the abyssal rock and the whole Fishing Trawler called stat_advance directly, so they paid no outfit
bonus and now would have rolled nothing either. They go through the procs. What is left calling
stat_advance directly for these seven skills is quest lump sums, every one of them, which is right:
finishing Heroes' Quest is not twenty hours of mining. Battery group 64 pins that line, so the next
skilling script cannot quietly step outside it.

Group 64's last check is the one that matters most: it RUNS tools/obtainable.py and asks whether it
still calls any of the 28 pieces unobtainable. The check and the code do not share an assumption -
that is the lesson from the magic stone, whose price check compared the price against the number I
had written. It also pins the four outfits still without a source (graceful, rogue, zealot's, guild
hunter) so that wiring one is a reminder to update it.

Twelve mutations, all red, each by the check it was written for.

ONE CORRECTION. The costume storage round said an inv cannot be handed to a proc and that nothing
in the repo does it, in the script header, the generator, the commit message and the project doc.
Both are false: ~inv_slotspace(inv $from_inv, inv $to_inv, enum $slots) in
general/scripts/misc/inv_procs.rs2 takes two, and has for as long as that file has existed. Five
invs and one set of procs was available and was not considered. The shape still stands on its own -
the item list has to be flat either way, because "take out a set" needs first/last windows into it -
but it stands on that, not on a limitation that does not exist. The claim is gone from all three
places it was written.

